          "type": "concept",
          "title": "The Antiquarian Origins (1500-1850)",
          "content": "Before archaeology was a science, it was a **hobby for the wealthy**. The first 'archaeologists' were **antiquarians** — gentlemen scholars who collected old objects for cabinets of curiosities.\n\nKey figures and moments:\n• **William Camden (1551-1623)** wrote *Britannia*, surveying ancient sites across England\n• **John Aubrey (1626-1697)** studied Stonehenge and Avebury\n• **Cyriacus of Ancona (1391-1453)** is sometimes called the 'father of archaeology' for documenting Greek and Roman ruins\n• **Pompeii's rediscovery (1748)** under King Charles III of Naples — but it was treasure-hunting, not science\n• **Napoleon's Egyptian campaign (1798)** brought scholars who documented monuments; the **Rosetta Stone** was found in 1799\n\nThe antiquarian era prized **objects over context**. A beautiful vase was the prize; the layer it came from was waste. This mindset still haunts museums full of objects with no recorded provenance.",
