          "explanation": "**1970** is the date of the **UNESCO Convention on the Means of Prohibiting and Preventing the Illicit Import, Export and Transfer of Ownership of Cultural Property**. It is the **operational cutoff date** for almost all modern antiquities ethics: any object that left its country of origin AFTER 1970 must have documented legal acquisition to be ethically transactable. Major museum associations (AAMD, ICOM, AAM) refuse to acquire post-1970 objects without solid provenance. Auction houses use 1970 as a key due diligence date. NAGPRA (1990) is critical but US-specific; the 1970 UNESCO Convention is the universal benchmark.",
