          "type": "concept",
          "title": "Ancient DNA: The Reich-Paabo Revolution",
          "content": "Until about **2010**, you could not reliably get DNA out of ancient bones. The molecules degrade, fragments are tiny, and modern human contamination was constant. Then a series of technical breakthroughs turned **ancient DNA (aDNA)** into archaeology's most powerful new lens.\n\n**Svante Paabo** (Max Planck Institute, Leipzig) won the **2022 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine** for sequencing the **Neanderthal genome** (2010) and discovering the **Denisovans** (2010) — an entirely new human lineage identified from a single finger bone in a Siberian cave. His lab proved that **non-African modern humans carry ~2% Neanderthal DNA** and that many East Asian and Oceanian populations carry **Denisovan DNA** as well.\n\n**David Reich** (Harvard Medical School) industrialized the technique. His lab has now sequenced **over 10,000 ancient genomes** — turning aDNA from a boutique research method into a population-scale tool.\n\n**What aDNA has revealed:**\n• **Yamnaya migration (~3000 BCE)** — a massive movement of steppe pastoralists from modern Ukraine/Russia swept across Europe, replacing or mixing with earlier farmers; brought Indo-European languages\n• **Bell Beaker turnover in Britain (~2500 BCE)** — ~90% of the British gene pool was replaced within a few centuries\n• **First Americans** — entered via Beringia in at least two waves; one ancestral group ('Ancient Beringians') stayed in Alaska while others spread south by ~15,000 BCE\n• **Polynesian voyaging** — DNA confirms South Pacific origin and detects early contact with South America (sweet potato genetics)\n• **Black Death** — the plague Yersinia pestis was sequenced from 14th-century teeth, identifying which variants killed in 1347-1351\n\n**How it works (briefly):**\n1. Drill into the **petrous bone** of the inner ear (best DNA preservation) or the dental cementum\n2. Extract under clean-room conditions\n3. Build a library of short DNA fragments (typically 30-70 base pairs)\n4. Sequence on Illumina platforms\n5. Map fragments to a modern reference genome\n6. Compare populations using SNPs (single-nucleotide polymorphisms)\n\n**Limitations and ethics:**\n• Destructive sampling — the bone fragment is consumed\n• Indigenous communities often must be consulted (post-NAGPRA, see Lesson 11)\n• 'Ghost populations' — sometimes we detect genetic signatures of groups we never find archaeologically\n• Risk of misuse for nationalist narratives (Reich has written extensively on this)",
