          "type": "practice",
          "title": "Practice: Write an Excavation Summary",
          "content": "**Exercise.** You have spent the season co-directing a small dig at a 1st-century-CE Roman villa in southern England. Your team opened three 5x5m trenches. Findings included a tessellated floor, a hypocaust (underfloor heating), several coins of Hadrian, two cremation burials in pottery vessels, and a substantial assemblage of samian ware (fine Roman ceramic).\n\nWrite a **350-word executive summary** for the season's site report. Use the structure modeled in this lesson.\n\n**Model answer.**\n\n**Marketon Villa Excavation, Summer 2025 — Executive Summary**\n\n**Site and context.** The Marketon Villa (NGR SU 234 567) is a small Romano-British villa first identified by geophysical survey in 2022. The 2025 season constituted the first systematic excavation. Three 5x5m trenches (T1-T3) were opened to test results of the gradiometer survey.\n\n**Methodology.** Single-context recording was employed throughout. All contexts were sampled for soil chemistry, charcoal, and small finds; bulk samples were taken from every closed context for flotation. Each trench was recorded by plan, section, and high-resolution photogrammetry. A Harris matrix was constructed in real time.\n\n**Key findings.** **T1** exposed a tessellated floor (Phase 3, c. 120-180 CE) overlying a hypocaust system in T2 — confirming the villa was a heated reception room rather than agricultural outbuilding. **T3** contained two cremation burials in coarse pottery vessels (Phase 2, late 1st CE), placed against a boundary wall that pre-dates the formal villa. Material culture included approximately 1,200 sherds of samian ware (predominantly Drag. 18/31 and 37 forms), nine coins of Hadrian and Antoninus Pius, and 47 nails consistent with timber-framed superstructure.\n\n**Specialist contributions.** Faunal analysis (Brown) identified domestic cattle, sheep, and pig with high beef proportions atypical for the region. Archaeobotanical assessment (Mehta) recovered spelt wheat, hulled barley, and unusually, peach stones — suggesting elite dietary access. Two radiocarbon dates on burial charcoal returned 70-130 CE (95% probability).\n\n**Interpretation.** Marketon was likely a **modest aspirational Romano-British elite residence**, occupied from the late 1st through late 2nd centuries CE. The pre-villa cremation burials suggest the family had connections to the site predating its formal Romanization. The peach stones and beef diet indicate access to long-distance trade networks.\n\n**Next steps.** 2026 season will extend T2 to determine the full extent of the heated wing and excavate a possible bath suite identified in 2025 magnetometry. Soil samples have been archived at the University of Reading for biomolecular analysis pending funding.\n\n**Key learning point:** A good summary names the **site, methods, finds, specialists, interpretation, and next steps** — in that order, concisely.",
