          "explanation": "Modern archaeology is fundamentally **interdisciplinary, scientific, and collaborative**. It draws on **field excavation** (Lessons 2-10), **chronometric and isotopic dating** (Lesson 2, 4), **ancient DNA** (Lesson 12), **remote sensing including satellite + LiDAR + AI** (Lesson 12), **specialist analyses** (osteology, archaeobotany, ceramics), and **ethical frameworks** including NAGPRA, repatriation, and partnership with descendant communities (Lesson 11). It is no longer Heinrich Schliemann's treasure-hunting, no longer the colonial museum-filling enterprise of the 19th century, and not a subset of text-based history — most of the human past has no texts. It is a science, but a humanistic one, that asks not just 'what happened?' but 'what did it mean, who claims it, and who gets to study it?'",
